Here I just have to object. Most people here are using threaded newsreaders. I
use a "traditional" text based, unix newsreader (slrn). It _does_ make use of
threads to display the messages, and so do most other "traditional" readers.
What makes ommission of context a pain for me is another thing: When I read a
group, all the messages I read are marked as such. Then when I leave that
group and reenter it at a later time, the read messages aren't displayed
anymore. If I want them, I have to tell slrn to reconstruct the entire thread
I want to read, just for the message the non-quoter is referring to. This is a
PITA. 

Not displaying read messages, though, is a *good* *thing*. It makes
reading a group *faster*. If the followups quote context, there is no problem
at all. Quoting context has been part of the netiquette since the beginning of
time. It isn't even much work with a good newsreader (Google groups makes it
much work). But with something like google groups, I have to actively look for
the messages I haven't read yet (even if they're marked in some way, this
costs time). It is just much more inconvenient, and I don't think this is just
my subjective opinion.

Google groups users, OTOH, don't seem to understand the concept of threads,
either. Technically, GG is threaded, but the way the threads are displayed
seems to be suboptimal, because more and more often you see GG users replying
to a seemingly random post in the thread, quoting (if they do) context from a
post not included in the 'References:' header (obviously by copy'n'paste).
That way the post appears in another subthread than the one it belongs when
viewed with a newsreader with a proper threading engine.

So saying that GG is "state of the art" and "traditional" newsreaders are
suboptimal, so that quoting context doesn't make sense, is complete nonsense
to me.
